{
  "type": "music",
  "id": "sampler",
  "version": 1,
  "note": "How the sampler plays recorded instruments (doc 24, Sampler rules). defaults apply to every instrument; byInstrument overrides any field for one instrument id. layers: bounds are the velocities (1..127) where one recorded layer hands over to the next (empty means evenly split), fadeWidth the number of velocity steps over which two layers crossfade (doc 24: 12), so a slow crescendo has no steps, blend how two recordings share a crossfade (pick: each strike sounds one of them, chosen with the blend's odds from a seeded stream, seed; mix: both sound together, which can dip the level because two strikes of one string are partly out of phase, measured October 2026: mix is smoothest on the Steinway, Kawai, Knight and FM piano, pick on the Yamaha upright, whose alternating takes differ most), and law the curve used when they mix: 0 a straight-line blend (right for recordings that are in phase), 1 equal power (right for unrelated ones); two recordings of one key sit between, so judge it by ear on the bench's close-up test. velocity: the attack loudness rebuilt from velocity because the library's files are normalized one by one: floorDb at velocity 1, topDb at 127, gamma the curve's shape (above 1 keeps soft playing soft longer). register: a gentle balance in dB (bassDb at the lowest A, trebleDb at the top C, 0 at pivotMidi). brightness: a lowpass that opens with velocity, lowHz at pp to highHz at f, never below minHarmonic times the note's fundamental. tuning: policy is recorded (as the piano was tuned, with its own stretch), equal (every zone pulled to equal temperament at A4 = 440 Hz, doc 24's rule) or stretch (pulled onto a gentle stretch curve, bassCents at the lowest A and trebleCents at the top C, 0 at pivotMidi); zones measured with less confidence than minConfidence stay as recorded. dampers: how fast a released key is stopped, a time constant from bassS to trebleS; keys at or above undampedFromMidi have no damper and ring on, as on a real grand. release: how loud the key-off sound plays relative to the note (dB) and how short notes make it louder. retrigger: a repeated key damps the old string over this many seconds (doc 24: 80 ms). stealing: the most voices at once per tier, and the fade when one is stolen (doc 24: 30 ms, never a click); decay rates guess each voice's current loudness. load: maxTailS trims long tails when files are decoded (0 keeps all), monoBelowMidi folds notes below that key to mono (0 for none), sets to load; both are 0 now because the Steinway's game set is already shaped (4 s tails, mono below C3) before encoding. pedalNoise: the recorded pedal mechanism (the Knight upright's pedal recordings, recipe music/instruments/pedal-knight) played under the piano on each press and lift, its level in dB, and on or off. exclude: recordings never to use, by file (a bad take, such as the Yamaha's G6 soft take that is mostly its second partial). Zones that have several takes of a layer play them in turn; a zone that lacks a layer uses its nearest one. Chosen by Matt on the bench, October 19, 2026: Steinway B, gentle stretch tuning, blend width 20 with curve 0.5 (mix), key-off sound -25 dB, the Knight's pedal sound on at -20 dB, dampers as the defaults, tails of 4 s, mono below C3, the middle five octaves, pedal and no-pedal recordings. byInstrument (October 19, 2026, the support kit): settings for the non-piano instruments, each replacing whole groups of the defaults. Sustained instruments (strings, solo cello, flute, bowed vibraphone, glass) play equal-tempered, with a soft attack and a release time (envelope: attackS, releaseS) instead of the piano's dampers, loop their held notes, never decay for voice stealing, and have no pedal sound. Plucked and struck instruments (harp, vibraphone) ring on (undampedFromMidi 0) and keep no key-off sound. Held notes are leveled by their loop's level (loopRmsDb) when the recipe has it, since a bowed or rubbed note swells in and its attack says little; glass corrects its pitch whatever the confidence (four untuned glasses placed at their nearest notes, up to 46 cents off). Strings bow in over 0.45 s and ring out over 1.4 s (Matt, October 19, 2026: the first beds were jarring), and are leveled by their held sound (loopRmsDb), not their first 300 ms, which on soft bowing is mostly bow noise. The bowed vibraphone is darker (lowpass 1.8 to 5.5 kHz) and 8 dB lower at the top (Matt, October 19, 2026: its high counter-line whined).",
